  message: "Not a duplicate. INV-zumin is about a multi-boundary PRIMITIVE losing all but one boundary to YAML row order (a catalogue-lookup defect). This item is about a measurement SERIES being incomparable to itself (an estimator/cohort-design defect). The SimHash collision is on shared measurement vocabulary — 'measured', 'population', 'VIOLATED', percentages — not on subject. Suppressing so the next reader is not sent to the wrong item."  # 965a
- op: discuss  # 7e89
  at: '2026-08-25T22:25:12Z'  # 7e89
  by: agent  # 7e89
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 7e89
  clock: 4  # 7e89
  nonce: '7e89'  # 7e89
  message: "OWNER RULING 2026-08-25, verbatim: \"Fix the measurement frame first\".\n\nChosen from three options put to the owner after they spotted the caddy\nconcentration themselves. The two NOT chosen, recorded so the choice keeps its\nmeaning: (1) keep recall stopped and continue precision work anyway, accepting\nthat the campaign cannot demonstrate its own effect; (2) lift the stop on the\ngrounds that the band's own condition -- \"until it is understood\" -- is\nsatisfied now that 0005 names the FP mechanisms.\n\nWHAT THIS RULING DOES:\n  * The P3 RE-RANK IS DEFERRED. It happens on a number produced under a fixed\n    frame, not on 0005.\n  * RECALL STAYS STOPPED meanwhile. WI-sivuz's band ruling stands until the\n    re-rank, and the re-rank has moved later, not away.\n  * The two questions put to the owner -- does recall stay stopped, and which\n    unit governs the band -- are NOT answered by this ruling. The unit question\n    becomes part of the frame deliverable rather than a standalone decision.\n\nA CONTAMINATION DISCLOSURE THAT CONSTRAINS HOW THE FRAME MAY BE BUILT, and it\napplies to me specifically. WI-sivuz's bands were pre-registered precisely so a\nlater interval could not argue them down. I have ALREADY COMPUTED how each\ncandidate estimator scores on 0005 (pooled 21.6%/10.0%, unweighted-5 10.7%/\n5.9%, unweighted-2 26.8%/14.8%, caddy-excluded 19.0%/16.7%). So an estimator I\nnow pick is chosen WITH KNOWLEDGE OF ITS RESULT, which is the exact failure\npre-registration exists to prevent. I therefore cannot ratify the frame myself.\nI can propose it with the sensitivity table attached and the reasoning visible;\nthe owner ratifies, the way WI-sivuz's bands were owner-ratified before any\ninterval existed.\n\nTHE ONE COMPONENT THAT ESCAPES THAT PROBLEM is the adjudication rubric: 0001's\nrubric already exists, was written before these populations, and is reused\nverbatim rather than re-chosen. It should be cited into the frame, not rewritten.\n\nNEXT, AND THE FIRST STEP IS THE GAP THIS ITEM ALREADY NAMED AS UNMEASURED:\nwhether a concentration-controlled cohort exists in the corpus at all, and what\nit would cost to adjudicate. A frame that names a cohort nobody can afford to\nadjudicate is not a frame. That survey is evidence-gathering, not a decision,\nso it proceeds without waiting on ratification."  # 7e89
- op: discuss  # 9bb8
  at: '2026-08-25T22:35:10Z'  # 9bb8
  by: agent  # 9bb8
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 9bb8
  clock: 5  # 9bb8
  nonce: 9bb8  # 9bb8
  message: "OWNER RATIFICATION 2026-08-25, verbatim, all three questions answered:\n\n  1. \"Budget — M=10, R=8\"\n  2. \"Unit — ratify situation as canonical\"\n  3. \"exclude and accept the series restarts at N=1\"\n\nTHE FRAME IS THEREFORE FIXED AS:\n  * UNIT: situation is canonical for the WI-sivuz band. The row rate is\n    reported beside it every time, never instead of it.\n  * ALLOCATION: exactly 10 situations from each of 8 qualifying repos = 80\n    adjudicated situations. Pooled rate and unweighted per-repo mean are the\n    same number by construction, so no repo can dominate and there is no\n    weighting scheme left to re-choose next measurement.\n  * COHORT: 0005's five repos (mitmproxy, caddy, apollo-server, express,\n    poetry) are EXCLUDED. The series restarts at N=1 and the first framed\n    measurement is comparable to nothing. That cost was named before the\n    choice and accepted.\n\nTWO CONSEQUENCES OF M=10 THAT WERE NOT VISIBLE WHEN THE NUMBER WAS CHOSEN, and\nthe first one is a feasibility risk rather than a detail.\n\n(A) M=10 RAISES THE QUALIFICATION BAR TO >=10 SITUATIONS PER REPO, because F4\nrule 1 is \"yields at least M situations\". Checked against the only per-repo\ndistribution that exists -- 0005's own cohort:\n\n    mitmproxy      14 situations   QUALIFIES\n    caddy          16 situations   QUALIFIES\n    apollo-server   2 situations   fails\n    express         3 situations   fails\n    poetry          2 situations   fails\n\nSo on the one distribution we can see, 2 of 5 repos clear M=10 -- and BOTH of\nthose two are excluded by the owner's answer to Q3. Finding 8 qualifying repos\nmay therefore require screening a large part of the ~500-repo corpus, and may\nnot succeed at all. This is not an argument against M=10 (a higher M buys\nper-repo stability, which is exactly what 0005's 2-and-3-situation repos\nlacked); it is a statement that the screen must be budgeted for breadth.\n\n(B) 80 SITUATIONS IS ~2.2x 0005'S ADJUDICATION LOAD (37), under the same\nfour-pass protocol (primary, blind, adjudication of disagreements, adversarial\nrefutation). The protocol is kept because on 0005 it demonstrably caught\nerrors; the cost scales with it.\n\nA FALLBACK, PRE-REGISTERED NOW RATHER THAN CHOSEN AFTER SEEING WHO QUALIFIES,\nwhich is the whole point of pre-registration. Proposed, NOT yet ratified:\n\n  If fewer than 8 repos qualify at M=10, HOLD R=8 AND REDUCE M, to the largest\n  value at which 8 repos qualify, with a FLOOR OF M=5. If 8 repos cannot be\n  found even at M=5, stop and report rather than relaxing the language-balance\n  rule or shrinking R.\n\nRATIONALE FOR THAT ORDER: the frame exists to control CONCENTRATION, and R is\nthe variable that controls it. Shrinking R to preserve M would trade away the\nthing being fixed to protect a number chosen for a secondary benefit. The M=5\nfloor is 0005-comparable in per-repo terms and is where the proposal started.\n\nThe owner is asked to ratify or overrule the fallback BEFORE the screen runs.\nAfter the screen I will know the per-repo distribution, and a fallback chosen\nthen is chosen with knowledge of which repos it admits."  # 9bb8
- op: discuss  # d064
  at: '2026-08-25T22:38:21Z'  # d064
  by: agent  # d064
  actor: jgstern_agent  # d064
  clock: 6  # d064
  nonce: d064  # d064
  message: "OWNER REVISION 2026-08-25, verbatim: \"ok you know what actually let's do\nM=5, R=16\". Supersedes the M=10 / R=8 ratification recorded above.\n\nSAME ADJUDICATION BUDGET, STRICTLY BETTER PROPERTIES. 5 x 16 = 80 situations,\nidentical to 10 x 8, so the four-pass protocol costs the same. What changes:\n\n  * CONCENTRATION CONTROL ROUGHLY DOUBLES. No repo can exceed 1/16 = 6.25% of\n    the pooled situations, against 1/8 = 12.5% at R=8 -- and against caddy's\n    43% of situations (75% of rows) in 0005, which is the defect this frame\n    exists to close.\n  * THE FEASIBILITY RISK LARGELY CLOSES. The qualification bar is F4 rule 1,\n    \">= M situations\", so it falls 10 -> 5. That was the risk flagged against\n    M=10: on 0005's distribution only 2 of 5 repos cleared 10, and both were\n    excluded by Q3.\n  * SCREENING COST MOVES IN TWO DIRECTIONS AT ONCE and the net is not\n    predictable from here: 16 qualifiers are needed instead of 8 (more\n    screening), but the bar is half as high (higher qualification rate). The\n    time trial now running is what prices it.\n\nONE THING THIS BREAKS, AND IT NEEDS RE-RATIFYING. F9's fallback read \"hold R=8\nand reduce M, floor M=5\". M IS NOW 5 -- the floor -- so that fallback is\nexhausted the moment it is invoked and provides no path at all.\n\nREVISED F9, PROPOSED, still pre-registered BEFORE the screen:\n\n  If fewer than 16 repos qualify at M=5, HOLD M=5 AND REDUCE R, to the largest\n  value achievable, with a FLOOR OF R=8. Below R=8, stop and report rather\n  than lowering M or relaxing language balance.\n\nRATIONALE FOR THE INVERTED ORDER. At M=10 the argument was that R controls\nconcentration so M should yield first. That argument does not survive M=5:\nbelow 5 situations per repo we re-create exactly the 0005 defect the frame\nexists to fix -- apollo-server at 2 situations and express at 3 were reported\nas \"0.0% precision\", which is a number a reader cannot distinguish from no\nsignal. So M=5 is a hard floor on INTERPRETABILITY, not a preference, and R\nbecomes the yielding variable. R=8 is a defensible floor because the owner\nratified R=8 as acceptable earlier today.\n\nA SECOND-ORDER OPTION, RAISED NOW BECAUSE IT IS STILL UNCONTAMINATED. F4 rule 3\ncaps any single language at half of R. At R=16 that permits 8 repos of one\nlanguage, which is a concentration of a different KIND from the one this frame\ncloses -- per-repo concentration is fixed, per-language concentration is not.\nR=16 makes a tighter cap feasible (e.g. no language above a third, ~5 of 16).\nI have NOT seen the corpus language distribution, so this proposal is clean;\nafter the screen it would not be. Recommend deciding it now or explicitly\ndeclining to."  # d064
- op: discuss  # 771b
  at: '2026-08-25T22:39:33Z'  # 771b
  by: agent  # 771b
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 771b
  clock: 7  # 771b
  nonce: 771b  # 771b
  message: "OWNER REVISION 2026-08-25, verbatim: \"so do M=7\". Taken literally: M=7 with\nR=16 unchanged, since R was not mentioned. Supersedes M=5/R=16.\n\nWHAT IT FIXES, and it is exactly what the preceding entry flagged as broken.\nAt M=5 the revised fallback had NO PATH: M was already at the interpretability\nfloor, so \"reduce M\" was exhausted on invocation and R had to yield\nimmediately, giving up concentration control at the first sign of shortfall.\nM=7 restores the ladder:\n\n    16 repos at M=7  ->  M=6  ->  M=5 (floor)  ->  only then reduce R (floor 8)\n\nSo the frame now degrades along the axis that costs least first (per-repo\nsample depth) and only touches concentration control as a last resort.\n\nTHE CONSEQUENCE THE OWNER MAY NOT HAVE INTENDED, flagged rather than assumed:\n7 x 16 = 112 SITUATIONS, against 80 under both previous settings. That is a\n40% increase in the adjudication budget, and adjudication is the only real cost\nin this frame -- four passes per situation (primary, blind, adjudication,\nadversarial). For reference 0005 adjudicated 37, so 112 is ~3x 0005.\n\nIf the intent was to hold the budget at 80 while raising M, the settings would\nbe M=7/R=11 (77) or M=7/R=12 (84) -- but BOTH cut R, which is the variable\ncarrying the concentration control this frame exists to provide, so I have NOT\nassumed that. Stated for the owner to confirm or correct.\n\nQUALIFICATION BAR rises with M: a repo now needs >= 7 situations. On 0005's\ndistribution that is still mitmproxy (14) and caddy (16) qualifying and\napollo-server (2) / express (3) / poetry (2) failing -- the same 2-of-5 split\nas M=5, because that distribution is bimodal rather than smooth. Whether the\nwider corpus is also bimodal is unknown and is what the screen measures.\n\nSCREENING COST MODEL, from the time trial now running (2 of 3 repos in):\n\n    pdfium-render   162 source files   145s   0 situations\n    fastapi        1242 source files   179s   0 situations\n\nCost is dominated by a FIXED ~140s, not by repo size -- 7.7x the files cost\n23% more time. So screening is ~3 min/repo, and total screen cost is driven by\nthe QUALIFICATION RATE, not by corpus size. Two consecutive zeros is a weak\nbut unwelcome early signal on that rate.\n\nMITIGATION AVAILABLE AND NOT YET APPLIED: the box has 6 cores and load ~1.3, so\nthe screen can run 4-way parallel. That turns a projected 10-hour serial screen\ninto ~2.5 hours and keeps the experiment-design playbook's 8-hour limit\nreachable even at a low qualification rate. Parallelism does not affect WHICH\nrepos are screened -- the F10 seeded order is unchanged -- so it carries no\nmethodological cost."  # 771b
- op: discuss  # e810
  at: '2026-08-25T22:42:05Z'  # e810
  by: agent  # e810
  actor: jgstern_agent  # e810
  clock: 8  # e810
  nonce: e810  # e810
  message: "SCREENING COST TRIAL COMPLETE 2026-08-25. Three repos, ~14x source-file range,\nall outside 0005's cohort. Artifacts: ~/hypergumbo_lab_notebook/frame_08252026/.\n\n    repo             source files   elapsed   situations\n    pdfium-render             162      145s            0\n    fastapi                  1,242      179s            0\n    harbor                   2,237      167s           43\n\nTWO RESULTS, AND THE SECOND IS THE ONE THAT MATTERS.\n\n(1) COST IS FLAT IN REPO SIZE. A 13.8x range in source files produced a 23%\nrange in wall clock, and the LARGEST repo was not the slowest. Screening is\n~2.8 min/repo essentially regardless of size, so total screen cost is set by\nthe QUALIFICATION RATE and by parallelism, not by which repos are in the\ncorpus. This kills the idea that big repos must be avoided to afford the screen.\n\n(2) THE SITUATION DISTRIBUTION IS BIMODAL, which the 0005 cohort already hinted\nat (14, 16, 2, 3, 2) and this confirms on unrelated repos: 0, 0, 43. Repos\nappear to produce either almost nothing or a lot. That has three consequences:\n\n  * The M threshold matters far less than it appeared to. M=5, M=7 and M=10 all\n    partition this distribution identically -- a repo clearing 7 will usually\n    clear 10, and one at 0 clears nothing. The earlier feasibility worry about\n    M=10 was real but smaller than it looked.\n  * The 1-in-3 qualification rate here (harbor) is a 3-repo estimate and must\n    not be treated as the rate. But it bounds the screen usefully: at 33%,\n    16 qualifiers need ~48 repos = ~2.2h serial; even at a pessimistic 10%,\n    ~160 repos = ~7.5h serial, or ~2h at the 4-way parallelism the box\n    supports. The screen is affordable under both.\n  * IT VALIDATES THE FRAME'S CENTRAL MECHANISM ON A REPO THAT IS NOT CADDY.\n    harbor alone yields 43 situations. Under 0005's pooled census that single\n    repo would have been ~54% of a 80-situation population -- the caddy defect\n    reproducing itself immediately on a fresh corpus. Under the ratified frame\n    harbor contributes exactly M=7 of 112, i.e. 6.25%. The cap is doing the\n    work it was designed to do, and the need for it is now demonstrated twice\n    on independent cohorts rather than argued from one.\n\nCONTAMINATION LEDGER, kept current: I have now seen situation counts for THREE\ncorpus repos (pdfium-render 0, fastapi 0, harbor 43) out of ~500. The cohort\nrule F4 and the screening protocol F10 remain effectively uncontaminated. That\nstops being true the moment the full screen runs, which is why F9/F10 and the\nper-language cap question are outstanding BEFORE it.\n\nNOT MEASURED: whether 0-situation repos are clean or merely under-recalled.\n0005 already disclaims recall (\"it is precision, not accuracy\"), and a repo\nreporting no violations may be either. It does not affect the frame -- such a\nrepo fails qualification either way -- but it must not be read as evidence\nabout those repos' safety."  # e810
- op: discuss  # bb61
  at: '2026-08-25T22:46:18Z'  # bb61
  by: agent  # bb61
  actor: jgstern_agent  # bb61
  clock: 9  # bb61
  nonce: bb61  # bb61
  message: "OWNER RATIFICATION 2026-08-25, verbatim: \"ratify F9 and F10, seed 20260825,\ncap language at a third\". THE FRAME IS NOW FULLY RATIFIED AND IN FORCE.\n\n  unit         situation canonical; row reported beside it every time\n  allocation   M=7, R=16  ->  112 adjudicated situations\n  cohort       0005's five excluded; the series restarts at N=1\n  F9           shortfall: step M 7 -> 6 -> 5 (floor), THEN reduce R (floor 8),\n               then stop and report. Never relax language balance.\n  F10          seeded screening order, SEED 20260825, stop at 16 admitted\n  language     no language above a THIRD of R -> max 5 of 16 (floor of 16/3)\n\nSCREEN LAUNCHED under those rules. 477 repos enumerated from ~/ALL_REPOS,\ndeduplicated by realpath across the symlinked cohorts, 0005's five removed\n(verified: zero matches in the order file), shuffled once on seed 20260825.\nAdmission walks the SEEDED ORDER; 4-way parallelism changes only which repos\nare screened concurrently, never which are admitted, so the order semantics are\nuntouched. Qualification is >=7 situations. Per-repo timeout 900s against a\n~170s observed typical.\n\nPRIMARY LANGUAGE is defined for the cap as the most-numerous source extension\namong taint-catalogued languages, excluding .git / node_modules / vendor /\n.venv. Declared here because it is a rule the cap depends on and it was not\nspecified in F4.\n\nCONTAMINATION LEDGER, closed out. Before this screen I had seen situation\ncounts for exactly three corpus repos (pdfium-render 0, fastapi 0, harbor 43),\nall from the cost trial. All three remain in the order file at whatever\nposition the seed gave them -- REMOVING them because I had seen them would\nitself be a selection effect. From the moment this screen completes I will know\nthe corpus distribution, so F4/F9/F10 could not be honestly re-opened by me\nafterwards. They were all ratified first, which is the property this sequencing\nexisted to protect.\n\nKNOWN RESIDUAL IN THE SCREEN, disclosed rather than discovered later: a repo\nthat TIMES OUT at 900s is recorded and not admitted. If a timeout would\notherwise have qualified, the cohort loses it. Observed typical is ~170s with\nno size dependence (13.8x files -> 23% time), so this should be rare, but it is\na selection effect and the count of timeouts must be reported with the cohort."  # bb61
- op: discuss  # ea51
  at: '2026-08-27T23:12:04Z'  # ea51
  by: agent  # ea51
  actor: jgstern_agent  # ea51
  clock: 10  # ea51
  nonce: ea51  # ea51
  message: "GATE 3 CLEARED. WI-gibom is done: 0006's useful precision is derived at 24.1% (27 of 112),\nnot carried forward as the underived <=25.0%. ADR-0046's \"not carried forward on authority\"\ncondition is satisfied, so a baseline series may now anchor to a checkable number.\n\nWHAT THE ANCHOR ACTUALLY IS, stated because a baseline must name its estimator:\n    correctness 33.9% (38/112)   useful 24.1% (27/112)\n    KIND-MISDECLARED 11   CONFIGURED-ACTION 0\nBoth figures share the denominator 112, so useful <= correctness by construction and the\ntwo are directly comparable, per ADR-0046.\n\nTWO THINGS THIS CHANGES FOR THE PRE-REGISTRATION:\n  1. CONFIGURED-ACTION cannot be assumed to bite. It contributed ZERO to 0006 and the\n     reason is structural: a claim set admitting only env_read / host_info_read sources\n     gives ADR-0046 clause 2 nothing to match. If INV-duvup's pre-registered claim set\n     resembles 0006's, the useful axis will move only with INV-nular. If the cohort is\n     meant to exercise the CONFIGURED-ACTION class at all, it needs config-file-driven\n     servers in it -- caddy's shape, which 0006 does not contain.\n  2. THE INSTRUMENT MOVED BETWEEN 0006 AND TODAY, and now by a measured amount. Six of\n     0006's eleven deductions are the bash \"> /dev/null\" defect PR #541 fixed the day\n     after the measurement. So 0006's useful figure is not comparable to a post-#541\n     measurement on the KIND-MISDECLARED axis. That is the same class of hazard this item\n     was filed about, arriving from a fix rather than from an estimator change: a\n     CORRECTNESS improvement that moves the number is indistinguishable from drift unless\n     the series records which class moved. ADR-0046 already requires that; the\n     pre-registration should say it explicitly.\n\nSTILL AHEAD OF THE FIRST DATUM (unchanged): INV-sukoh (P3, +5.3% row denominator) and the\nINV-nular / INV-fumod remainders, both partially landed and still violated. The pending\npython/elixir vocabulary ruling inside INV-nular is now sized on 0006's population --\nresolving it against the current rows takes useful precision from 24.1% to 10.7% -- so it\nis a bigger lever on the useful axis than anything else outstanding."  # ea51
